1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,1000
Funders reject more budgets for one quiet reason than for asking for too much money.

2
00:00:04,1000 --> 00:00:07,024
The numbers don't match the story.

3
00:00:07,024 --> 00:00:13,273
Your proposal promises one thing, your budget adds up to another, and the reviewer notices before you do.

4
00:00:13,273 --> 00:00:16,785
Stick around and I'll hand you the finished template, free.

5
00:00:16,785 --> 00:00:20,773
But first, let's build a grant budget that actually holds together.

6
00:00:20,773 --> 00:00:25,059
If you've ever stared at a blank budget form the night before a deadline

7
00:00:25,059 --> 00:00:29,820
padding round numbers until the total looked about right, this video is for you.

8
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:33,034
A grant budget isn't a wish list and it isn't a guess.

9
00:00:33,034 --> 00:00:37,439
It's a financial argument, and every line is a claim a reviewer can check.

10
00:00:37,439 --> 00:00:42,082
So over the next eleven minutes, let's build a grant budget from a blank sheet

11
00:00:42,082 --> 00:00:46,308
the way a reviewer reads it and the way an auditor will check it later.

12
00:00:46,308 --> 00:00:48,688
The finished template is genuinely free.

13
00:00:48,688 --> 00:00:55,176
Drop your email at the link in the description and I'll send you both the Google Sheets and the Excel version

14
00:00:55,176 --> 00:00:58,748
with every line and the budget narrative tab already set up.

15
00:00:58,748 --> 00:01:00,890
Quick note on where I'm coming from.

16
00:01:00,890 --> 00:01:02,855
I build grant compliance software

17
00:01:02,855 --> 00:01:06,962
so I'm going to show you the structure that survives a federal review

18
00:01:06,962 --> 00:01:09,223
not just the one that fills the boxes.

19
00:01:09,223 --> 00:01:12,438
Everything here works in plain Google Sheets or Excel.

20
00:01:12,438 --> 00:01:13,747
No add-ons, no macros.

21
00:01:13,747 --> 00:01:16,266
Picture the budget most people hand in.

22
00:01:16,266 --> 00:01:18,203
A column of big round numbers.

23
00:01:18,203 --> 00:01:22,659
Fifty thousand for staff, ten thousand for supplies, five for travel.

24
00:01:22,659 --> 00:01:25,694
It looks clean, and that's exactly the problem.

25
00:01:25,694 --> 00:01:29,956
Round numbers tell a reviewer you estimated instead of calculated.

26
00:01:29,956 --> 00:01:33,120
A grant budget fails in three predictable places.

27
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:36,220
First, the math doesn't tie back to the proposal

28
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:41,192
so the activities you described don't line up with the dollars you asked for.

29
00:01:41,192 --> 00:01:47,908
Second, there's no indirect cost line, so you're quietly funding your own overhead out of program money.

30
00:01:47,908 --> 00:01:53,591
Third, there's no narrative, no sentence anywhere explaining how you got to each number.

31
00:01:53,591 --> 00:01:56,626
By the end of this, we'll have fixed all three.

32
00:01:56,626 --> 00:01:57,401
Let's build.

33
00:01:57,401 --> 00:01:58,531
Open a new sheet.

34
00:01:58,531 --> 00:02:02,384
Before you type a single dollar, you set up the categories

35
00:02:02,384 --> 00:02:06,570
because funders expect the same ones in roughly the same order.

36
00:02:06,570 --> 00:02:10,224
Get the skeleton right and the rest is just filling in.

37
00:02:10,224 --> 00:02:15,872
These are the standard federal cost categories, and most foundations follow them too.

38
00:02:15,872 --> 00:02:16,536
Personnel.

39
00:02:16,536 --> 00:02:17,599
Fringe benefits.

40
00:02:17,599 --> 00:02:18,064
Travel.

41
00:02:18,064 --> 00:02:18,729
Equipment.

42
00:02:18,729 --> 00:02:19,327
Supplies.

43
00:02:19,327 --> 00:02:20,124
Contractual.

44
00:02:20,124 --> 00:02:20,523
Other.

45
00:02:20,523 --> 00:02:23,047
And then indirect costs at the bottom.

46
00:02:23,047 --> 00:02:28,761
The reason this order matters is that a reviewer reads dozens of budgets in a sitting.

47
00:02:28,761 --> 00:02:31,951
When yours uses the categories they already know

48
00:02:31,951 --> 00:02:36,801
they can find what they're looking for and trust that you know the rules.

49
00:02:36,801 --> 00:02:44,043
A budget organized your own clever way just makes them work harder, and a tired reviewer is not on your side.

50
00:02:44,043 --> 00:02:46,501
Down the left, list those categories.

51
00:02:46,501 --> 00:02:51,750
Across the top, three columns to start: a description, the math, and the total.

52
00:02:51,750 --> 00:02:57,331
The math column is the one almost everyone skips, and it's the one that earns trust.

53
00:02:57,331 --> 00:02:59,723
We'll come back to it on every line.

54
00:02:59,723 --> 00:03:04,979
Personnel is almost always your largest category, and it's where reviewers look first.

55
00:03:04,979 --> 00:03:07,485
So we calculate it, we never estimate it.

56
00:03:07,485 --> 00:03:11,886
For each person on the grant, you need three things: their annual salary

57
00:03:11,886 --> 00:03:16,837
the percentage of their time the grant pays for, and the math that connects them.

58
00:03:16,837 --> 00:03:23,315
Say your program director earns eighty thousand a year and spends a quarter of their time on this project.

59
00:03:23,315 --> 00:03:28,816
That's eighty thousand times twenty-five percent, so twenty thousand dollars to the grant.

60
00:03:28,816 --> 00:03:34,011
Write that math in the description, the salary and the percent of effort, every time.

61
00:03:34,011 --> 00:03:36,945
The percent of effort is a real compliance term.

62
00:03:36,945 --> 00:03:41,345
It's the share of a person's total working time that this award pays for

63
00:03:41,345 --> 00:03:45,502
and on a federal grant you'll later have to certify it was accurate.

64
00:03:45,502 --> 00:03:51,552
So the number you put here is a promise you're making about how someone actually spends their week.

65
00:03:51,552 --> 00:03:54,242
Right under personnel comes fringe benefits.

66
00:03:54,242 --> 00:04:00,965
Fringe is the real cost of employing someone beyond their salary: payroll taxes, health insurance, retirement.

67
00:04:00,965 --> 00:04:04,815
You express it as a percentage of the salaries you're charging.

68
00:04:04,815 --> 00:04:07,994
If your organization's fringe rate is thirty percent

69
00:04:07,994 --> 00:04:13,311
and you charged twenty thousand in salary to this grant, that's six thousand in fringe.

70
00:04:13,311 --> 00:04:16,795
Don't fold benefits into salary and don't leave them out.

71
00:04:16,795 --> 00:04:17,956
Both are red flags.

72
00:04:17,956 --> 00:04:22,479
Your finance person knows your real fringe rate, so use that, not a guess.

73
00:04:22,479 --> 00:04:27,165
Now the rest of your direct costs, the things you buy to run the program.

74
00:04:27,165 --> 00:04:30,054
Travel, supplies, contractual, and equipment.

75
00:04:30,054 --> 00:04:35,511
The trick on every one of these is the same: break it into units, never one lump sum.

76
00:04:35,511 --> 00:04:37,822
Travel isn't "five thousand dollars.

77
00:04:37,822 --> 00:04:44,370
" It's three staff, to one conference, at a known airfare, plus hotel nights at a real per-night rate.

78
00:04:44,370 --> 00:04:49,570
Supplies is the number of laptops times the price, not "supplies, eight thousand.

79
00:04:49,570 --> 00:04:54,963
" Contractual is for the consultants and subcontractors doing defined pieces of work

80
00:04:54,963 --> 00:04:56,825
each with a rate and a scope.

81
00:04:56,825 --> 00:05:00,420
When you show the units, a reviewer can sanity-check you

82
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:04,593
and passing that check is how you build credibility line by line.

83
00:05:04,593 --> 00:05:09,087
One distinction trips people up constantly: equipment versus supplies.

84
00:05:09,087 --> 00:05:10,563
Under the federal rules

85
00:05:10,563 --> 00:05:16,855
equipment is anything that costs ten thousand dollars or more per unit and lasts more than a year.

86
00:05:16,855 --> 00:05:19,166
Below that threshold, it's a supply.

87
00:05:19,166 --> 00:05:24,558
This matters more than it sounds, because in a minute we'll calculate indirect costs

88
00:05:24,558 --> 00:05:28,410
and equipment gets treated differently from everything else.

89
00:05:28,410 --> 00:05:31,299
So a three-hundred-dollar tablet is a supply.

90
00:05:31,299 --> 00:05:34,894
A twelve-thousand-dollar piece of lab gear is equipment.

91
00:05:34,894 --> 00:05:39,709
Put each in the right row now and the indirect math works itself out later.

92
00:05:39,709 --> 00:05:45,066
Here's the line most nonprofits leave money on the table by skipping: indirect costs.

93
00:05:45,066 --> 00:05:50,234
These are your real costs of operating that you can't tie to one specific program.

94
00:05:50,234 --> 00:05:52,755
Rent, utilities, accounting, the lights.

95
00:05:52,755 --> 00:05:56,285
The grant should help pay for them, and if you don't ask

96
00:05:56,285 --> 00:05:59,625
you're subsidizing the funder out of your own pocket.

97
00:05:59,625 --> 00:06:03,974
If you've never negotiated a formal indirect rate with the government

98
00:06:03,974 --> 00:06:07,503
you're allowed to use what's called the de minimis rate.

99
00:06:07,503 --> 00:06:14,562
And here's a number a lot of older videos still get wrong: as of the 2024 update to the federal Uniform Guidance

100
00:06:14,562 --> 00:06:18,659
that de minimis rate went up from ten percent to fifteen percent.

101
00:06:18,659 --> 00:06:23,071
You apply it to something called modified total direct costs, or MTDC.

102
00:06:23,071 --> 00:06:25,088
That's most of your direct costs

103
00:06:25,088 --> 00:06:31,768
but it deliberately leaves out equipment and the part of any single subaward above fifty thousand dollars.

104
00:06:31,768 --> 00:06:36,054
So you total your eligible direct costs, multiply by fifteen percent

105
00:06:36,054 --> 00:06:39,962
and that's a legitimate indirect line no funder can argue with

106
00:06:39,962 --> 00:06:42,546
because it's written into the regulation.

107
00:06:42,546 --> 00:06:45,760
I know this is the driest two minutes of the video.

108
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:52,126
It's also the part that quietly puts thousands of dollars back into your organization on every grant.

109
00:06:52,126 --> 00:06:57,357
The template does this calculation for you, so you set your rate once and it flows.

110
00:06:57,357 --> 00:07:00,911
Some grants ask you to bring your own money to the table.

111
00:07:00,911 --> 00:07:02,781
That's cost sharing, or match.

112
00:07:02,781 --> 00:07:08,517
The funder covers part, you cover the rest, and you have to show where your part comes from.

113
00:07:08,517 --> 00:07:10,200
Match comes in two flavors.

114
00:07:10,200 --> 00:07:14,066
Cash match is real dollars from your budget or another funder.

115
00:07:14,066 --> 00:07:18,804
In-kind match is the value of things donated to the project: volunteer hours

116
00:07:18,804 --> 00:07:20,862
donated space, pro bono services.

117
00:07:20,862 --> 00:07:24,104
Both count, but here's the rule that catches people.

118
00:07:24,104 --> 00:07:26,535
If you commit to a match in your budget

119
00:07:26,535 --> 00:07:30,089
you are legally on the hook to deliver it and document it

120
00:07:30,089 --> 00:07:33,269
the same way you document the grant's own spending.

121
00:07:33,269 --> 00:07:36,511
So only put down match you can actually prove later.

122
00:07:36,511 --> 00:07:40,252
And one more thing worth knowing: on federal research grants

123
00:07:40,252 --> 00:07:46,985
a funder can't use voluntary match as a scoring factor unless a statute and the funding notice both allow it

124
00:07:46,985 --> 00:07:49,853
so don't pad your match hoping to look better.

125
00:07:49,853 --> 00:07:53,781
Match what's required, document what you match, and stop there.

126
00:07:53,781 --> 00:07:58,880
Now the piece that wins or loses more budgets than the math: the budget narrative.

127
00:07:58,880 --> 00:08:01,181
Also called the budget justification.

128
00:08:01,181 --> 00:08:07,648
It's a short written explanation, line by line, of how you got each number and why the project needs it.

129
00:08:07,648 --> 00:08:09,762
Most people treat it as paperwork.

130
00:08:09,762 --> 00:08:12,125
Reviewers treat it as the whole point.

131
00:08:12,125 --> 00:08:13,306
The rule is simple.

132
00:08:13,306 --> 00:08:16,042
Every number in your budget gets a sentence.

133
00:08:16,042 --> 00:08:17,784
Not a paragraph, a sentence.

134
00:08:17,784 --> 00:08:22,882
"Program director at twenty-five percent effort to supervise curriculum and staff.

135
00:08:22,882 --> 00:08:28,044
" "Three round-trip flights to the national convening at four hundred dollars each.

136
00:08:28,044 --> 00:08:32,707
" "Fifteen percent de minimis indirect rate on modified total direct costs.

137
00:08:32,707 --> 00:08:39,174
" When a reviewer reads a number and immediately finds the sentence that explains it, two things happen.

138
00:08:39,174 --> 00:08:42,035
They believe the number, and they believe you.

139
00:08:42,035 --> 00:08:47,382
A budget without a narrative is just a reviewer's list of questions you didn't answer.

140
00:08:47,382 --> 00:08:51,673
The template gives you a narrative tab already structured by category

141
00:08:51,673 --> 00:08:54,160
so you're never staring at a blank page.

142
00:08:54,160 --> 00:08:59,819
You fill in one sentence per line as you build the budget, not at midnight before it's due.

143
00:08:59,819 --> 00:09:03,991
Two compliance guardrails before we talk about where this breaks.

144
00:09:03,991 --> 00:09:07,072
The first is allowable versus unallowable costs.

145
00:09:07,072 --> 00:09:12,143
Federal rules are explicit that some things simply cannot be charged to a grant

146
00:09:12,143 --> 00:09:14,390
no matter how reasonable they feel.

147
00:09:14,390 --> 00:09:14,904
Alcohol.

148
00:09:14,904 --> 00:09:15,481
Lobbying.

149
00:09:15,481 --> 00:09:16,637
Fundraising costs.

150
00:09:16,637 --> 00:09:17,856
Most entertainment.

151
00:09:17,856 --> 00:09:22,414
If it's on the unallowable list, it doesn't go in the budget, full stop

152
00:09:22,414 --> 00:09:27,806
and slipping one in is the kind of thing that turns a routine review into a finding.

153
00:09:27,806 --> 00:09:30,373
The second is the period of performance.

154
00:09:30,373 --> 00:09:35,380
That's the window the grant actually covers, the official start and end dates.

155
00:09:35,380 --> 00:09:40,387
Money you spend before the start date or after the end date can get disallowed

156
00:09:40,387 --> 00:09:43,853
even when the expense itself was perfectly reasonable.

157
00:09:43,853 --> 00:09:48,347
So your budget should only contain costs that fall inside that window.

158
00:09:48,347 --> 00:09:53,225
It sounds obvious, and it's one of the most common things that gets flagged.

159
00:09:53,225 --> 00:09:59,227
I build grant software for a living, so let me be honest about where this template stops working.

160
00:09:59,227 --> 00:10:01,146
Because at some point, it will.

161
00:10:01,146 --> 00:10:04,611
A budget template is built for one moment: the proposal.

162
00:10:04,611 --> 00:10:05,601
It's a snapshot.

163
00:10:05,601 --> 00:10:12,408
The trouble starts the day the grant is awarded and the budget has to become a living thing you track against.

164
00:10:12,408 --> 00:10:14,822
The first crack is budget versus actual

165
00:10:14,822 --> 00:10:20,391
keeping spent amounts current against this plan across a whole year, by category, by hand.

166
00:10:20,391 --> 00:10:26,208
The second is when one cost has to split across two grants, and no clean formula allocates it.

167
00:10:26,208 --> 00:10:27,755
The third is just volume.

168
00:10:27,755 --> 00:10:32,272
A few grants, each with its own budget, its own period, its own narrative

169
00:10:32,272 --> 00:10:35,985
and the spreadsheet quietly becomes someone's full-time job.

170
00:10:35,985 --> 00:10:38,151
None of that is you doing it wrong.

171
00:10:38,151 --> 00:10:42,173
It's a static template doing exactly what a static template does.

172
00:10:42,173 --> 00:10:47,766
When you hit that wall, the next step is software that keeps the budget alive after the award.

173
00:10:47,766 --> 00:10:50,027
Spent tracked against plan on its own.

174
00:10:50,027 --> 00:10:53,240
The indirect rate applied the same way on every grant.

175
00:10:53,240 --> 00:10:55,858
Restricted funds kept separate by the system

176
00:10:55,858 --> 00:11:00,499
so the budget you proposed and the money you actually spend never drift apart.

177
